    "parts":  [
                  "Your salt does not need a date. Neither does your chewing gum.",
                  "In Europe, the food labelling law has a list of foods that can skip it.",
                  "The list includes cooking salt, vinegar and solid sugar.",
                  "Drinks with ten percent alcohol or more.",
                  "Bread and pastries that are normally eaten within twenty-four hours.",
                  "And fresh fruit and vegetables, if they have not been peeled or cut.",
                  "That last part is the twist.",
                  "A whole apple needs no date.",
                  "Our reading: cut it, pack it, and it is off the list. Then the usual date rules apply.",
                  "One warning. No date does not mean it never goes bad.",
                  "It only means the law does not ask for one.",
                  "So why does everything else carry two different dates?",
                  "The full story is in the video linked below."
              ],
